  "worked_solution": "First, we use the product rule for radicals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{12} & =\\sqrt{4\\cdot 3}=\\sqrt{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}=2\\sqrt{3} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\sqrt{3}-5\\sqrt{12} & = \\\\ 4\\sqrt{3}-5(2\\sqrt{3}) & = \\\\ 4\\sqrt{3}-10\\sqrt{3}. & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can combine the two $\\sqrt{3}$ terms together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\sqrt{3}-10\\sqrt{3} & = \\\\ (4-10)\\sqrt{3} & = \\\\ -6\\sqrt{3} & \\end{aligned}\n$$",
